---
title: Welcome
description: Learn how to configure feature flags in the Vonage Video Android App.
---

Welcome! In this tutorial you will configure the **Vonage Video Android App** (`vonage-video-android-app`), a Kotlin application built with Gradle. Building and running it natively requires **Android Studio Ladybug (2024.2.1)+**, **JDK 17+**, and an emulator or physical device — none of which this Codespace has. So instead of editing and building here, you will clone the repo to your own machine and do all of that work there.

This Codespace's only job is to run the shared Node.js backend server that the Android app talks to, and to expose its port so your locally-running app can reach it.

## In this tutorial you will

- Start the shared backend server in this Codespace and make its port public
- Clone `vonage-video-android-app` to your own machine and run it in Android Studio against that backend
- Explore how feature flags are structured in `config/app-config.json`
- Edit feature flags and regenerate the corresponding Kotlin configuration
- Rebuild the app and confirm your changes in the Android emulator

{% aside type="caution" %}
This Codespace cannot run Android Studio or an emulator. You cannot build, run, or preview the Android app here — everything you do in this Codespace is limited to running the backend server. Building and verifying the app happens in Android Studio on your own machine.
{% /aside %}

{% aside %}
Looking to customize colors, typography, or shape instead? See the companion **Vonage Video Android App Theme Customization** tutorial.
{% /aside %}

Let's start by setting up the backend server and getting the app running locally.
